from owned_build import *
# Per-object decisions, made individually from each named manual and inventory.
data='''
/etc/DIR_COLORS|NOT|dir_colors.5|Colors filenames displayed by ls|The program ls(1) uses the environment variable LS_COLORS|S2.2-7,S2.2-13|Only display colors inside ls change; values are settings, not executable shell code.
/etc/DIR_COLORS.lightbgcolor|NOT|dir_colors.5|Supplies an alternate ls color theme|configuration file for dircolors(1)|S2.2-7,S2.2-13|An alternate display-color theme is an exempt program setting.
/etc/adjtime|MUST|adjtime.5|Stores hardware clock mode and drift correction used across clock utilities|hardware mode clock setting and clock drift factor|S2.2-1,S2.2-2|Clock interpretation and correction affect system time across programs, not merely one program’s internal presentation.
/etc/aliases|UNKNOWN|aliases.5|Defines mail aliases, potentially delivery addresses or executed commands|an alias can refer to other aliases|S2.2-4,S2.2-10,S3-2|Without contents, ordinary address data cannot be distinguished from command-delivery aliases; documented format supports both.
/etc/bashrc|MUST|bash.1|Executes shell startup commands with shell privileges|executes commands read from the standard input, from a string, or from a file|S2.2-10,S2.2-13|Shell startup files are explicitly required even when configuring shell behavior.
/etc/csh.cshrc|MUST|csh.1|Executes system shell initialization commands|C shell with file name completion and command line editing|S2.2-10,S2.2-13|System shell startup code is explicitly required by the shell-startup example.
/etc/csh.login|MUST|csh.1|Executes login-shell initialization commands|C shell with file name completion and command line editing|S2.2-10,S2.2-13|Login startup commands execute with shell rights, so the explicit code rule applies.
/etc/host.conf|MUST|host.conf.5|Configures shared resolver behavior for applications|configuration information specific to the resolver library|S2.2-1,S2.2-2|Shared name-resolution behavior affects multiple programs; the single-program settings exemption does not apply.
/etc/hosts|MUST|hosts.5|Maps network names to addresses across applications|associates IP addresses with hostnames|S2.2-1,S2.2-2|Removing configured resolution mappings can make named network destinations unavailable across applications.
/etc/exports|MUST|exports.5|Controls exported filesystems and client access|Each file system in this table has a list of options and an access control list.|S2.2-1,S2.2-2|Filesystem export permissions govern what other clients can access, beyond one program’s internal settings.
/etc/group|MUST|group.5|Defines groups and group membership|defines the groups on the system|S2.2-1,S2.2-15|Group identities and memberships determine account access rights across the system.
/etc/passwd|MUST|passwd.5|Defines local accounts and login shells|describes user login accounts for the system|S2.2-15|The text explicitly requires accounts; this file defines them.
/etc/gshadow|HOLE|gshadow.5|Combines group passwords, membership, and administrative rights|contains the shadowed information for group accounts|S2.2-1,S2.2-15|Rights require recording while passwords need not; the text does not specify how to classify a single file containing both.
/etc/shadow|HOLE|shadow.5|Stores account passwords together with lock and expiration controls|contains the password information for the system's accounts and optional aging information|S2.2-15|Passwords are exempt but login restrictions are required; no sentence specifies whole-file treatment when both occupy the same object.
/etc/ld.so.conf|MUST|ld.so.conf.5|Configures shared-library search paths|a list of directories, one per line, in which to search for libraries|S2.2-1,S2.2-2|Library discovery enables multiple programs to load their dependencies.
/etc/libaudit.conf|MUST|libaudit.conf.5|Configures audit failure behavior shared by linked applications|configuration information for user space applications that link to libaudit|S2.2-1,S2.2-2|Policy controls several applications through a shared library, exceeding the single-program exemption.
/etc/locale.conf|MUST|locale.conf.5|Sets system-wide locale defaults|configures system-wide locale settings|S2.2-1,S2.2-13|Locale values apply across system processes, not only inside one program.
/etc/login.defs|MUST|login.defs.5|Defines account-management and authentication defaults|defines the site-specific configuration for the shadow password suite|S2.2-1,S2.2-15|Account-management defaults and authentication controls operate across the password-management suite.
/etc/logrotate.conf|HOLE|logrotate.8|Configures rotation and processing of system logs|allows automatic rotation, compression, removal, and mailing of log files|S2.2-1,S2.2-7,S2.2-13|One reading exempts values consumed by logrotate; another requires their externally visible file-management behavior. The text does not define inside.
/etc/machine-id|MUST|machine-id.5|Provides persistent system identity shared by programs|contains the unique machine ID of the local system|S2.2-1,S2.2-2|A shared operational identity affects multiple consumers rather than merely describing display content.
/etc/mke2fs.conf|HOLE|mke2fs.conf.5|Chooses filesystem-creation parameters|controls the default parameters used by mke2fs(8)|S2.2-1,S2.2-7,S2.2-13|Values configure one program but determine externally created filesystems; the text does not delimit settings in force inside one program.
/etc/networks|MUST|networks.5|Provides network names to shared lookup functions|describes known DARPA networks and symbolic names for these networks|S2.2-1,S2.2-2|Network-name resolution is shared operational behavior, not merely the display of stored content.
/etc/nfs.conf|MUST|nfs.conf.5|Sets shared configuration for NFS daemons and utilities|site-specific configuration for various NFS daemons and other processes|S2.2-1,S2.2-2|Documented settings coordinate multiple NFS processes, outside the single-program exemption.
/etc/nfsmount.conf|HOLE|nfsmount.conf.5|Sets NFS mount defaults|section headers followed by variable assignments associated with that section|S2.2-1,S2.2-7,S2.2-13|Mount-helper values affect external mounted filesystems, but one-program settings are exempt; the boundary of inside is unspecified.
/etc/protocols|MUST|protocols.5|Maps network protocol names to numbers for applications|describing the various DARPA internet protocols|S2.2-1,S2.2-2|Name-based protocol selection is shared operational behavior; removing mappings removes that capability.
/etc/request-key.conf|MUST|request-key.conf.5|Selects helpers invoked to instantiate requested kernel keys|determine which program it should run to instantiate a key|S2.2-1,S2.2-2|Helper selection governs kernel key availability and invokes other programs, beyond an internal preference.
/etc/rpc|MUST|rpc.5|Maps RPC program names to numeric identifiers|names that can be used in place of RPC program numbers|S2.2-1,S2.2-2|Removing name mappings disables name-based RPC lookups across callers.
/etc/rsyncd.conf|MUST|rsyncd.conf.5|Controls remotely accessible modules and authentication|controls authentication, access, logging and available modules|S2.2-1,S2.2-2|Rules decide which remote access is possible, beyond internal presentation settings.
/etc/sestatus.conf|NOT|sestatus.conf.5|Chooses security-context information displayed by sestatus|determine what file and process security contexts should be displayed|S2.2-7,S2.2-13|Only the reporting program’s output selection changes; these are internal display settings.
/etc/shells|MUST|shells.5|Lists permitted login shells used by account and access tools|full pathnames of valid login shells|S2.2-1,S2.2-15|The shell list controls login-related access and account management across programs.
/etc/subgid|MUST|subgid.5|Delegates group-ID ranges for child namespaces|authorizes a group id to map ranges of group ids|S2.2-1,S2.2-2|Removing delegation changes which namespace identities an account may use.
/etc/subuid|MUST|subuid.5|Delegates user-ID ranges for child namespaces|authorizes a user id to map ranges of user ids|S2.2-1,S2.2-2|Removing delegation changes which namespace identities an account may use.
/etc/sudo.conf|MUST|sudo.conf.5|Selects privilege-management policy and audit plugins|used to configure the sudo front-end|S2.2-1,S2.2-15|Privilege-granting mechanisms implement the explicitly required right to become superuser.
/etc/sudoers|MUST|sudoers.5|Defines allowed privilege elevation|determines a user's sudo privileges|S2.2-15|The text explicitly requires the right to become superuser.
/etc/sysctl.conf|MUST|sysctl.conf.5|Stores kernel parameter settings|contain Linux kernel configuration parameters|S2.2-1,S2.2-2|Kernel parameters determine system-wide behavior rather than settings confined to one program.
/etc/vconsole.conf|MUST|vconsole.conf.5|Sets console keyboard mapping and font|configures the virtual console, i.e. keyboard mapping and console font|S2.2-1,S2.2-2|The console input and display configuration applies across programs using the console.
/etc/issue|NOT|issue.5|Provides text displayed before login|contains a message or system identification to be printed before the login prompt|S2.2-4|Removing the message removes displayed content, not the login capability.
/etc/issue.net|NOT|issue.5|Provides network-login banner text|contains a message or system identification|S2.2-4|Banner text is content; its absence does not itself remove authentication capability.
/etc/motd|NOT|motd.5|Displays a post-login message|displayed by login(1) after a successful login|S2.2-4|Only the message content becomes unavailable.
/etc/os-release|HOLE|os-release.5|Publishes operating-system identification values|contain operating system identification data|S2.2-1,S2.2-4|Identification is readable content, but software can branch on it; the text gives no boundary between descriptive content and behavioral input.
'''
manuals={}
for line in data.strip().splitlines():
 name,dec,man,what,quote,sent,reason=line.split('|');u='https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man'+man.rsplit('.',1)[1]+'/'+man+'.html'
 manuals.setdefault(u,[]).append(quote)
 row=next(r for r in rows if r['name']==name)
 append(row,dec,sent,what,'doc:'+u,reason)
notes=base/'part-1.owned.notes.md'
with notes.open('a') as f:
 f.write('# Package-owned path classification\n\nMembership is the normalized intersection of `files.tsv` and `package-files.txt`, applying `path-aliases.tsv`. Nested input groups are excluded from ancestor groups. All 237 member counts agree after normalization. Package ownership is never a recording criterion. Filesystem contents were not collected: the raw file table supplies type, path, link target, and size only.\n\n## Directory-only rule\n\nFor rows consisting solely of directory entries, the inventory establishes a namespace container but not operational consumers or reconstruction behavior. General directory-purpose documentation does not establish these instances’ actual functional dependence. These are UNKNOWN under S3-2, not a presumption that empty directories are content. No physical removal is performed.\n\nManual checks: coreos-3cd182c07b, coreos-827c5914e9, coreos-037231a728, coreos-d3d071d2ba, coreos-c0faa16997, coreos-43399114f3, coreos-edd98ae366, coreos-fc1b65b090, coreos-31a0673933, coreos-1ae98d3e98. Each has one member with raw type d and no supplied contents or consumer trace.\n\nGeneral source: https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/hier.7.html — “A typical Linux system has, among others, the following directories:” This describes typical layout, not operational use on this instance.\n\n## Individually consulted sources\n\n')
 for u,quotes in manuals.items():f.write('- '+u+' — '+ '; '.join('“'+q+'”' for q in dict.fromkeys(quotes))+'\n')
 f.write('\nEach single-file entry above was decided individually; the script only serializes the written decisions.\n')
print(collections.Counter(r['decision'] for r in csv.DictReader(out.open(),delimiter='\t')))
